2026-08-31          v1.0.0

                    First release, and the first accurate entry in
                    this file: the v0.1.0 note below was written at
                    the initial commit and never touched again, so
                    it describes a much smaller product than the one
                    that shipped. Everything here is in addition to
                    it.

                    MESSAGES. Replies, editing, emoji reactions,
                    read markers, typing indicators, unread counts
                    and mentions. Photos, videos, voice notes, files
                    and locations — sent alone or as an album of up
                    to ten — with per-family deduplication, so the
                    same picture sent twice is stored once. Polls. A
                    family board of sticky notes. Birthdays. A
                    per-family language.

                    CALLS. One-to-one voice and video between family
                    members: peer-to-peer WebRTC, with the server
                    passing offers, answers and ICE candidates and
                    never media. They ring like real calls — CallKit
                    on iOS, Telecom on Android — and an optional
                    TURN relay covers networks that will not carry a
                    direct connection. Video can be turned off
                    separately from voice, because a relay sized for
                    voice is not sized for video.

                    PUSH NOTIFICATIONS ACTUALLY DELIVER. v0.1.0
                    collected device tokens and logged what it would
                    have sent; this release ships working APNs (with
                    PushKit VoIP for incoming calls) and FCM
                    transports. Note the default: [push]
                    include_message_body = true puts the message
                    text into the notification, which means Apple or
                    Google see it. Set it to false if that is not
                    the trade you want.

                    AN OPTIONAL ASSISTANT. Off unless the operator
                    fills in [ai]. Each member gets one private
                    assistant chat that no other member can read,
                    and the assistant can also be mentioned by name
                    in the family chat. What leaves the server is
                    enumerated in docs/protocol.md and nothing else
                    does — but it does leave the server, to
                    whichever provider the operator configured.

                    SAFETY AND MODERATION. Any member can block
                    another, silently, without the blocked member
                    being told. Any member can report a member or a
                    specific message to the family owner. Owners can
                    reset a member's password, choose whether an
                    invite code joins immediately or needs approval,
                    close the family entirely, and cap its size.
                    Ownership passes on rather than trapping an
                    owner who wants to leave.

                    ACCOUNTS AND HISTORY. Permanent account
                    deletion, profile pictures, family statistics,
                    and a retention sweep that deletes messages and
                    their attachments past a configurable age.

                    APPS. A native macOS app alongside iOS, from one
                    universal build (iOS 17+, macOS 14+), plus the
                    Android app (Android 8+).

                    SERVER. Thirty migrations, applied at startup.
                    Rate limiting at nginx on the two
                    unauthenticated argon2 endpoints, a bound on
                    concurrent password hashes, cgroup memory
                    limits, and a free-space floor that refuses
                    uploads before PostgreSQL runs out of room. A
                    backup script and timer, and a tested restore
                    procedure, in docs/operations.md.

                    The server binary is 1.0.0. It reported 0.1.0
                    throughout all of the above.

2026-08-19          v0.1.0

                    Initial version. Self-hosted family chat: Rust
                    server (Axum + PostgreSQL, REST + WebSocket API,
                    opaque session tokens, families with invite codes
                    and owner approval, family-wide chat plus 1:1
                    member chats, push-notification hooks without a
                    transport yet), SwiftUI iOS client (iOS 17+) and
                    Jetpack Compose Android client (Android 8+), both
                    with offline history caches, optimistic sending
                    with retry, reconnect resync, unread counts, read
                    markers and typing indicators.
